			"Message": "docs(Cocoon): Restructure README; normalize dash characters and section headings\n\nReorder the README document flow so the \"Getting Started\" section — including prerequisites, build instructions, dependency table, and debugging notes — appears before \"Security\" rather than after it. This aligns the Cocoon README with the standard project-documentation hierarchy (overview → features → architecture → setup → security → API reference).\n\nAlso normalize markdown formatting: replace typographic long dashes (—) with regular hyphens (-) throughout the feature bullet lists, and rename the \"Architecture\" heading to \"System Architecture\" for consistency with the broader Land documentation convention.\n\nNo runtime logic, identifiers, or module structure was modified.",
			"SHA": "8a69dedc5ac5c3c2f0bfef75719c6648c043fdd3"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-06-12T20:04:38Z",
			"File": [
				"⣿⡟⠀⠀⠀",
				561,
				194
			],
			"Message": "style(Cocoon): Remove blank lines after opening braces; normalize doc formatting\n\nRemove the blank line immediately following opening braces across all interface, type, enum, class, function, and conditional declarations in the Cocoon source tree, scripts, Proto definitions, services, handler modules, IPC layer, telemetry, type converters, webview panels, and tests — reversing the whitespace convention established in commit 553b7052.\n\nAlso normalize markdown formatting in DeepDive.md and README.md: realign tables with proper pipe-positioned column separators, reflow prose paragraphs to consistent line widths, and clean up table-of-contents indentation.\n\nNo runtime logic, identifier names, or module structure was modified.",
			"SHA": "85e19690ff82aea5116568ba96185ef7a0cfebb7"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-06-12T19:12:04Z",
			"File": [
				"⣿⡟⠀⠀⠀",
				561,
				191
			],
			"Message": "style(Cocoon): Normalize blank-line spacing across interface, enum, function, and conditional blocks\n\nAdd a consistent blank line after opening braces in all interface, type, enum, class, and function declarations — as well as after ternary conditionals (`?`) and arrow-function bodies (`=>`) — to bring the entire codebase into a single whitespace convention.\n\nThe change touches ~180 files across the Cocoon source tree, Proto definitions, scripts, services, handler modules, IPC layer, telemetry, type converters, webview panels, and tests. No runtime logic, identifier names, or module structure was modified.\n\nEvery statement, expression, and declaration block now follows the same vertical spacing rule, eliminating intra-file inconsistencies that previously mixed compact and spaced styles within the same module.",
			"SHA": "553b7052d4263309d3c2d5e9f59c883622e51d92"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-06-12T18:23:04Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				561,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Cocoon): Dispose partial extension context on activation failure\n\nWhen ActivateExtension throws mid-activation, an extension may have already pushed disposables onto context.subscriptions before the error. The old catch block deleted the extension from ActiveExtensionContexts without disposing those registered disposables, leaking watchers and providers.\n\nStore the partial RegisteredContext in ActiveExtensionContexts so DisposeExtensionContext iterates and calls .dispose() on every registered disposable, then removes the map entry. Shared cleanup now handles both successful and failed activation paths uniformly.",
			"SHA": "2bc0675c8e91772a928c5df07b065f88f148de7a"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-06-12T18:20:47Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				561,
				3
			],
			"Message": "feat(Cocoon): Track extension contexts for subscription disposal on deactivation; fix machineId fallback chain\n\nStore each activated extension's `ExtensionContext` in `ActiveExtensionContexts` so that when an extension is deactivated — via host re-initialization (`HandleInitializeExtensionHost`) or delta extension removal (`HandleDeltaExtensions`) — every disposable the extension registered via `context.subscriptions` is iterated and `.dispose()` called, preventing leaked watchers, status-bar items, and language providers from the prior host generation.\n\n**ActivateExtension.ts:**\n- Add `ActiveExtensionContexts` map keyed by extension id, populated at the `PromoteToActivated` step\n- Add `DisposeExtensionContext()` helper that clears `subscriptions` and drops the context entry\n- Capture `RegisteredContext` before `activate()` so it is available to `PromoteToActivated` even after a successful `activate()` call\n- Clean up `ActiveExtensionContexts` on activation failure alongside the existing `ActivatingExtensions`/`ActivatedExtensions` cleanup\n\n**Handler.ts:**\n- On host re-init: iterate all active contexts and dispose them before clearing the registry, mirroring the prior deactivateExtension logic but at the batch level\n- On delta removal: call `DisposeExtensionContext` and remove from `ActivatedExtensions` so a re-installed extension can activate fresh\n\n**Env/Namespace.ts:**\n- Fix the `machineId` fallback chain: `Env['machineId']` → `telemetryInfo.machineId` → `telemetry.machineId` → `'fiddee'`, restoring the correct priority order after a previous reordering broke field resolution\n\nExtension contexts are now correctly torn down on host lifecycle transitions, and the machineId field resolves from the richer `telemetryInfo` payload before falling back to the legacy `telemetry` object.",
			"SHA": "2b90036c1bdac36e69436b05fea4b6c27be72836"
		}
	],
	"TimeStamp": 1782051969735
}